## IMPORTANT
- Only what was asked, in the fewest lines that fully solve it; reuse existing code first.
- Prove every change with the narrowest test. New behaviour gets a test that asserts exact
  expected values, never values recomputed with the code under test.
- Read only the lines you need, once per task; re-read a file only if it changed.
- Never read or print secrets; never commit `.env`, keys or tokens.
- Replies: the answer, then at most one short follow-up.

## Who does what
- Do small jobs yourself (a known file, a test run, an edit); helpers cost more than they save there.
- `explorer` (Haiku): 5+ files or a long output. `reviewer` (Haiku): diffs over ~50 lines or
  touching security, data or money. `debugger`: a bug two fixes haven't solved.
- `planner` (Opus): plan multi-file work. `designer` (Opus): a whole page people will see, in
  one pass; don't build such pages yourself. `big-decision` (rare): costly-to-reverse choices.
- `researcher`: web or docs lookups. A helper refused permission: do that step yourself.

## Where to look (only when needed)
- Finding code: `CODEMAP.md`; run `/map` after adding or moving files.
- Dead code: `python scripts/find_unused.py [folder]`. Big files: `python scripts/find_large.py`,
  then `split-file`.
- Same correction twice, or "always/never": add a rule to `.claude/LEARNED.md`. Same
  multi-step job twice: offer to save it as a skill (`learn-corrections`).

